Q: draggable modifier prevents item selection A: <p>I found a solution, I moved the <code>.draggable</code> modifier to the <code>Label</code> of the <code>DisclosureGroup</code>:</p>
<pre><code>struct FolderRow: View {
    @Environment(\.modelContext) private var context

    var folder: Folder

    @State private var isExpanded: Bool = true

    var body: some View {
        DisclosureGroup(isExpanded: $isExpanded) {
            // Subfolders
            ForEach(folder.subfolders) { subfolder in
                FolderRow(folder: subfolder)
            }

            // Entries (leaf nodes)
            ForEach(folder.entries) { entry in
                NavigationLink(destination: EntryDetail(entry: entry)) {
                    EntryRow(entry: entry)
                }
                .draggable(entry.name)
            }
        } label: {
            Label(folder.name, systemImage: &quot;folder&quot;)
                .draggable(folder.name) // &lt;&lt;&lt;==== MOVE HERE
        }
        .dropDestination(for: String.self) { names, _ in
            return handleDrop(of: names)
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Virtual range initializer in C++ to be used in range-based for loop A: <p>You can't sensibly return pointers from <code>begin</code> and <code>end</code>, because you can't provide an overload of <code>==</code> for two pointer types, nor can you get an <code>int</code> from the dereference of such a pointer that <code>for</code> is defined to use.</p>
<p>If you have subclasses <code>B</code> and <code>C</code> with different containers, you could have <code>virtual boost::any_range&lt;int&gt; getTestRangeInitializer() = 0</code> in <code>A</code>. However it could well be quicker to copy the values into a <code>std::vector&lt;int&gt;</code>, and return that.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>class A {
public:
virtual std::vector&lt;int&gt; getTestRangeInitializer() = 0;
};

class B : public A {
std::list&lt;int&gt; data;
public:
std::vector&lt;int&gt; getTestRangeInitializer() override {
    return { std::from_range, data };
}
};

class C : public A{
std::set&lt;int&gt; data;
public:
std::vector&lt;int&gt; getTestRangeInitializer() override {
    return { std::from_range, data };
};
};
</code></pre>

Q: shadow_root - find_elements vs find_element and nth-child() A: <p>The issue is <strong>not timing</strong>. <code>:nth-child()</code> fails inside Shadow DOM because of how Selenium's CSS selector engine evaluates pseudo-selectors within shadow roots — not a bug in your code.</p>
<p><code>:nth-child()</code> counts <strong>all siblings</strong> from the parent, not just elements matching your selector. Inside Shadow DOM, this counting mechanism breaks down further because Selenium's selector engine cannot properly traverse the encapsulated DOM tree for complex pseudo-selectors.</p>
<p><strong>Solution 1: JavaScript <code>querySelectorAll</code> + Index</strong></p>
<p>Bypass Selenium's selector engine entirely and use the browser's native JS engine directly:</p>
<pre><code>def get_nth_shadow_element(driver, shadow_host, css_selector, nth_index):
    script = &quot;&quot;&quot;
        const host = arguments[0];
        const selector = arguments[1];
        const index = arguments[2];
        const shadowRoot = host.shadowRoot;
        if (!shadowRoot) return null;
        const elements = shadowRoot.querySelectorAll(selector);
        return elements[index] || null;
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    return driver.execute_script(script, shadow_host, css_selector, nth_index)

# zero-based, so 4 = 5th element
el = get_nth_shadow_element(driver, shadow, &quot;span.fifty-radio__text&quot;, 4)
log.info(el.get_attribute('outerHTML'))
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Solution 2: Search by Text Content</strong></p>
<p>More robust than index-based — won't break if DOM order changes:</p>
<pre><code>def get_shadow_element_by_text(driver, shadow_host, css_selector, text):
    script = &quot;&quot;&quot;
        const root = arguments[0].shadowRoot;
        if (!root) return null;
        const elements = root.querySelectorAll(arguments[1]);
        const searchText = arguments[2].trim().toLowerCase();
        return Array.from(elements).find(
            el =&gt; el.textContent.trim().toLowerCase().includes(searchText)
        ) || null;
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    return driver.execute_script(script, shadow_host, css_selector, text)

el = get_shadow_element_by_text(driver, shadow, &quot;span.fifty-radio__text&quot;, &quot;Timeseries graph&quot;)
log.info(el.get_attribute('outerHTML'))
</code></pre>

Q: Extending class and assigning different value to an existing property A: <p>The problem with the <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79915792/27900156">other answer</a> is that the type annotation <code>/** @type {'a'|'b'|'c'} */</code> cannot be reused. You have to repeat it, and that violates the S.P.O.T. principle (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_source_of_truth" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Single Point of Truth</a>). This is not good at all.</p>
<p>To make type annotation work as you expected, you can exolicitely declare <code>typedef</code> to reuse it:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>/**
 * @typedef { 'a' | 'b' | 'c' } ValidLetters
 */

class X {
    constructor() {
        /** @type {ValidLetters} */
        this.property = 'a';
    }
}

class Y extends X {
    constructor() {
        super();
        /** @type {ValidLetters} */
        this.property = 'b';
        // this.property = 'e'; // will not pass validation
    }
}

// test:

const x = new X();
console.log(x.property); // a

const y = new Y();
console.log(y.property); // c
</code></pre>
<p>Better yet, you can declare the property explicitly with the same JSDoc type constraint. Then it will be inherited:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>/**
 * @typedef { 'a' | 'b' | 'c' } ValidLetters
 */

class X {
    /** @type {ValidLetters} */
    property = 'b';
    constructor() {
        this.property = 'a';
    }
}

class Y extends X {
    constructor() {
        super();
        // no need to repeat the type:
        this.property = 'b';
        //this.property = 'e'; // will not pass validation
    }
}

// test:

const x = new X();
console.log(x.property); // a

const y = new Y();
console.log(y.property); // c
</code></pre>
<p>Note that the JSDoc type checking is only the validation. If the validation fails (as in the case when you uncomment <code>this.property = 'e';</code>) the script will execute successfully.</p>
